sident of the Alliance of Sahel States (AES) from 2024 to 2025.\nBorn in Bamako, Goïta graduated from the Joint Military School in 2002 and was later assigned to the 134th Reconnaissance Squadron in Gao.","image":"Assimi Goïta in July 2023.jpg","birth":1983.854794520548,"death":null},"Q113577661":{"label":"Abdoulaye Maïga","description":"Interim Prime Minister of Mali","enwiki":"Abdoulaye Maiga (officer)","summary":"Abdoulaye Maiga (born 12 May 1981) is a Malian military officer and politician who is serving as the interim prime minister of Mali since November 2024, previously serving in the position from August to December 2022.\nMaiga is a lieutenant colonel in the Malian National Gendarmerie, which is a military force with law enforcement duties among the civilian population and is part of the Malian armed forces.","image":"Deputy PM Abdoulaye Maïga in 2024 (cropped).png","birth":1981.358904109589,"death":null},"Q170597":{"label":"Amadou Toumani Touré","description":"Malian soldier and politician","enwiki":"Amadou Toumani Touré","summary":"Amadou Toumani Touré (4 November 1948 – 9 November 2020), also popularly known by his initials ATT (French pronunciation: [atete]), was a Malian politician and military officer who served as Mali's head of state twice, from 1991 to 1992 as acting president and again as a democratically elected president from 2002 until he was deposed in a coup in 2012.\nTouré was head of President Moussa Traoré's personal guard (and parachute regiment) when a popular revolution and a coup overthrew the regime in March 1991. Touré then arrested Traoré and led the revolution onward. He presided over a year-long military-civilian transition process that produced a new Constitution and multiparty elections, then handed power to Mali's first democratically elected president, Alpha Oumar Konaré, on 8 June 1992.","image":"Amadou Toure.jpg","birth":1948.8415300546449,"death":2020.8551912568305},"Q313239":{"label":"Alpha Oumar Konaré","description":"President of Mali (1992 to 2002)","enwiki":"Alpha Oumar Konaré","summary":"Alpha Oumar Konaré (born 2 February 1946) is a Malian politician, professor, historian and archaeologist, who served as President of Mali for two five-year terms from 1992 to 2002 and was Chairperson of the African Union Commission from 2003 to 2008.","image":"Konare27022007.jpg","birth":1946.0876712328768,"death":null},"Q313240":{"label":"Modibo Keïta","description":"Malian politician and President of Mali (1915-1977)","enwiki":"Modibo Keïta","summary":"Modibo Keïta (4 June 1915 – 16 May 1977) was a Malian politician who served as the first President of Mali from 1960 to 1968. He espoused a form of African socialism. He was deposed in a coup d'état in 1968 by Moussa Traoré.\nBorn and raised in Bamako, Keïta began a career as a teacher in 1936 under French colonial rule before entering politics during the 1940s.","image":"JFKWHP-KN-C18793-A (cropped2).jpg","birth":1915.4219178082192,"death":1977.3698630136987},"Q313242":{"label":"Moussa Traoré","description":"Malian former dictator (1936-2020)","enwiki":"Moussa Traoré","summary":"Moussa Traoré (25 September 1936 – 15 September 2020) was a Malian military officer, politician, and dictator who served as the second President of Mali from 1968 to 1991. As a lieutenant, he led the military ousting of President Modibo Keïta in 1968. Thereafter he served as head of state until March 1991, when he was overthrown by popular protests and a military coup.\nDuring his tenure, political activity was banned, marking a shift to authoritarian control.","image":"Moussa Traoré (1989) (cropped).jpg","birth":1936.7322404371585,"death":2020.704918032787},"Q324108":{"label":"Ibrahim Boubacar Keïta","description":"Malian politician","enwiki":"Ibrahim Boubacar Keïta","summary":"Ibrahim Boubacar Keïta (French: [ibʁa.im bubakaʁ ke.ita]; 29 January 1945 – 16 January 2022), often known by his initials IBK, was a Malian politician who served as the president of Mali from September 2013 to August 2020, when he was forced to resign in the 2020 Malian coup d'état. He served as Mali's prime minister from February 1994 to February 2000 and as president of the National Assembly of Mali from September 2002 to September 2007.\nKeïta founded the centre-left political party Rally for Mali (RPM) in 2001. After a number of unsuccessful campaigns, he was elected president in the 2013 presidential election and reelected in 2018.","image":"Ibrahim Boubacar Keïta par Claude Truong-Ngoc décembre 2013 (cropped).jpg","birth":1945.0767123287671,"death":2022.041095890411},"Q372679":{"label":"Amadou Sanogo","description":"Malian military officer","enwiki":"Amadou Sanogo","summary":"Amadou Haya Sanogo (born 1972 or 1973) is a Malian military officer who was leader of the 2012 Malian coup d'état against President Amadou Toumani Touré. He proclaimed himself the leader of the National Committee for Recovering Democracy and Restoring the State (CNRDRE). Sanogo was also said to be involved in the arrest and resignation of acting Prime Minister Cheick Modibo Diarra in December 2012, leading to the appointment of civil servant Django Sissoko as Prime Minister. According to Human Rights Watch, Sanogo’s forces were implicated in serious human rights abuses including torture, sexual abuse, and intimidation against journalists and family members of detained soldiers.","image":"Amadou Haya Sanogo December 2016.jpg","birth":1972.9125683060108,"death":null},"Q372780":{"label":"Dioncounda Traoré","description":"President of Mali","enwiki":"Dioncounda Traoré","summary":"Dioncounda Traoré (born 23 February 1942) is a Malian politician who was President of Mali in an interim capacity from April 2012 to September 2013. Previously he was President of the National Assembly of Mali from 2007 to 2012, and he served as Minister of Foreign Affairs from 1994 to 1997. He was President of the Alliance for Democracy in Mali-African Party for Solidarity and Justice (ADEMA-PASJ) beginning in 2000, and he was also President of the Alliance for Democracy and Progress (ADP), an alliance of parties that supported the re-election of President Amadou Toumani Touré in 2007.","image":"Dioncounda Traore photo officielle de campagne 2 Mali 2012.jpg","birth":1942.145205479452,"death":null},"Q99522144":{"label":"Bah Ndaw","description":"President of Mali","enwiki":"Bah Ndaw","summary":"Bah Ndaw (also spelled N'Daw, N'Dah, and N'Daou; born 23 August 1950) is a Malian retired military officer and politician who served as the interim president of Mali from 2020 to 2021, when he was deposed in a coup. He previously served as the Minister of Defense and Veterans Affairs in the government of President Ibrahim Boubacar Keïta from 2014 to 2015.","image":"Visit of Josep Borrell Fontelles, Vice-President of the European Commission, to Mali.jpg","birth":1950.641095890411,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of Mali.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Flag_of_Mali.svg/330px-Flag_of_Mali.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mali.svg","author":"SKopp","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Mali.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Coat_of_arms_of_Mali.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Mali.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Mali.svg","author":"UnknownUnknown , vectorized by Perhelion","license":"Public domain"},"Assimi Goïta in July 2023.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Assimi_Go%C3%AFta_in_July_2023.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Assimi_Go%C3%AFta_in_July_2023.jpg","author":"President of Russia,Kremlin","license":"CC BY 4.0"},"Deputy PM Abdoulaye Maïga in 2024 (cropped).png":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Deputy_PM_Abdoulaye_Ma%C3%AFga_in_2024_%28cropped%29.png/330px-Deputy_PM_Abdoulaye_Ma%C3%AFga_in_2024_%28cropped%29.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deputy_PM_Abdoulaye_Ma%C3%AFga_in_2024_(cropped).png","author":"VOA Africa","license":"Public domain"},"Amadou Toure.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Amadou_Toure.jpg/330px-Amadou_Toure.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amadou_Toure.jpg","author":"LulaAmadouToure.jpg: Antonio Cruz/ABr\nderivative work: Missionary (talk)","license":"CC BY 2.5 br"},"Konare27022007.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Konare27022007.jpg/330px-Konare27022007.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konare27022007.jpg","author":"Fábio Pozzebom/ABr","license":"CC BY 3.0 br"},"JFKWHP-KN-C18793-A (cropped2).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/JFKWHP-KN-C18793-A_%28cropped2%29.jpg/330px-JFKWHP-KN-C18793-A_%28cropped2%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JFKWHP-KN-C18793-A_(cropped2).jpg","author":"Robert LeRoy Knudsen","license":"Public domain"},"Moussa Traoré (1989) (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c7/Moussa_Traor%C3%A9_%281989%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moussa_Traor%C3%A9_(1989)_(cropped).jpg","author":"Rob Bogaerts for Anefo","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 nl"},"Ibrahim Boubacar Keïta par Claude Truong-Ngoc décembre 2013 (croppe